# ponytail: the Content-Security-Policy below is the REAL one. A <meta http-equiv>
# policy ignores frame-ancestors and cannot cover anything the browser fetched
# before it parsed the tag, so the policy is served as a header. The Astro
# dev/preview server never reads this file, so src/layouts/BaseLayout.astro
# ALSO emits the same policy as a <meta> tag. Both strings come from
# src/data/csp.ts, and `node scripts/check-csp.mjs` fails if they drift.
#
# Netlify's _headers cannot interpolate a build-time env var (only
# netlify.toml supports placeholder substitution), so the header's connect-src
# cannot name the deploy-time API origin. It allows any https origin instead,
# and the <meta> tag -- which CAN read PUBLIC_API_URL -- carries the strict
# one. A browser enforces both policies (the effective policy is the
# intersection), so production stays exactly as strict as before and the
# header can never silently break login/checkout.

# ponytail: max-age is one year -- the shortest value the preload list accepts,
# and long enough that a returning visitor stays protected for a year (a short
# max-age is a rollout value, not a policy). includeSubDomains extends the
# policy to the API host: HSTS is host-scoped, so without it a subdomain
# served over plain HTTP is still strippable. The `preload` token only makes
# the domain ELIGIBLE -- the actual preload happens when krt.design is
# submitted at hstspreload.org, and THAT is the irreversible step: removal is
# a slow, manual request and browsers keep the entry for months, so a
# subdomain that is not HTTPS-only becomes unreachable over HTTP for the whole
# max-age. Keep the token now (it changes nothing until the submission),
# submit the domain only once every subdomain is HTTPS for good. This is also
# exactly the header Netlify already serves, so production does not change.

# ponytail: X-Frame-Options stays even though frame-ancestors 'none' now
# covers framing -- it still protects clients that do not implement CSP
# frame-ancestors, and it costs one line.

# ponytail: NO Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy. Every asset this site serves is
# same-origin (og cards, brand, self-hosted fonts) and nothing user-supplied
# is served as a file, so CORP buys nothing here -- but the og/*.png cards are
# consumed by OTHER sites (Telegram, Slack, WhatsApp unfurls), and a
# browser-based preview renderer would be blocked by CORP: same-origin and
# the share image would go blank. Rejected deliberately; revisit only if the
# site ever starts serving user-generated files.
#
# ponytail: NO Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy. COEP breaks every cross-origin
# subresource. The site loads none today, but a wrong COEP is a silent,
# hard-to-debug breakage (Google Fonts, Stripe, any future embed).
#
# ponytail: Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin IS safe here: the site
# opens no popup (no window.open, no target="_blank", no window.opener), and
# it severs the cross-origin opener relationship that tabnabbing abuses.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https:; object-src 'none'; frame-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self';
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), browsing-topics=(), interest-cohort=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
